The economic  effect of an  allocation is  substantial  if there is a reasonable
possibility that it will  substantially  affect the amount to be received by the
members from the limited liability company, independent of tax consequences.  An
economic effect is not  substantial if, at the time the allocation  becomes part
of the operating  agreement:  (1) at least one member's after-tax return may, in
present value terms, be enhanced compared to his or her return if the allocation
were  not  contained  in the  operating  agreement;  and (2)  there  is a strong
likelihood  that no member's  after-tax  return will, in present value terms, be
substantially  diminished  compared to his or her return if the allocation  were
not  contained in the operating  agreement.  The IRS  regulations  on this issue
state that, in determining  after-tax  return,  a member's entire tax situation,
including aspects unrelated to the limited liability company, will be taken into
account.

